WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language:  en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
Archival voice: Used with no dye or fillers…
[Music]

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:06.212
Narrator: We live in an age of plastics.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.878
From the ocean shore to the Himalayas,

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:12.611
the planet is littered with bags, bottles,

00:00:12.611 --> 00:00:14.720
and other objects born from plastic.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.720
At times, it seems plastic trash will&nbsp;
haunt us for centuries, if not millennia.&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.160
Yet, not all plastic leftovers&nbsp;are durable—or worthless.&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.240
In fact, museum conservators are fishing for ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.840
to prevent priceless plastic&nbsp;artifacts from breaking down.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.800
Nearly every museum in the&nbsp;world houses plastic objects:&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.640
Vintage phones, Disney films,&nbsp;the first artificial heart—&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.694
these and other plastic artifacts

00:00:45.694 --> 00:00:49.280
are made of&nbsp;synthetic polymers that are vulnerable to decay.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.320
Unstable plastics can be found in&nbsp;things like early billiard balls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.423
old Legos,

00:00:57.937 --> 00:00:59.920
and foam art&nbsp;masterpieces from the ‘60s.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.666
Over time, these plastic materials—

00:01:04.318 --> 00:01:06.812
and even fairly stable ones like PET,

00:01:06.812 --> 00:01:10.149
found in water bottles and food packaging—can degrade,

00:01:10.149 --> 00:01:14.000
sometimes oozing out&nbsp;additives and melting into sludge.&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.960
Oxygen, heat, and light are&nbsp;often implicated in this carnage,&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.240
but different plastics respond&nbsp;
differently to such environmental factors.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.160
Because plastics come in so many varieties,&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.600
conservators sometimes can’t tell exactly&nbsp;
what materials are in their collections,&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.751
which can hinder efforts to preserve artifacts.

00:01:36.978 --> 00:01:39.630
Some conservators have used sensory data,

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.840
such as the sound, feel, and smell of plastics
to tease out the materials’ identities.&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.680
A vinegary scent, for instance,&nbsp;signals cellulose acetate.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.040
Archival voice: Celluloid, one&nbsp;of the earliest of plastics…

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.400
Conservators also have turned&nbsp;to controlled experiments&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.760
to get a grasp on how plastic artifacts degrade.&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.680
One study simulated the plastic&nbsp;fabrics in modern raincoats.&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.960
You might think that storing them at the coldest&nbsp;
possible temperature would best preserve them.&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.135
But these fabrics turned brittle

00:02:11.135 --> 00:02:14.160
and were&nbsp;pocked with holes after deep-freeze storage.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.680
Storing them at a milder 4.4 degrees&nbsp;Celsius prevented this problem.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.720
Still, running experiments to help&nbsp;
preserve museum objects can be tricky.&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.480
Over the decades, modern plastic manufacturers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.920
have switched up their&nbsp;recipes for making polymers.&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.840
They now add ingredients such as&nbsp;stabilizers and flame retardants.&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.339
Old plastics didn’t contain those ingredients,

00:02:39.339 --> 00:02:42.360
so conservators who want to save older polymers

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.949
need to make historically accurate versions

00:02:44.949 --> 00:02:47.411
to test out their conservation methods.

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:51.144
With this in mind, scientists at the Getty&nbsp;Conservation Institute

00:02:51.144 --> 00:02:54.177
paged through old trade magazines and dug up

00:02:54.177 --> 00:02:56.880
the exact&nbsp;ingredients of past plastics recipes.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.440
After mimicking the recipes,&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.931
the scientists are now running stress&nbsp;tests

00:03:02.238 --> 00:03:04.000
on their new old plastics

00:03:04.365 --> 00:03:07.280
with an eye toward artificial&nbsp;aging tests down the line.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.680
Saving plastic artifacts is&nbsp;more than a matter of nostalgia.&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.083
These objects may serve as time capsules

00:03:15.083 --> 00:03:18.400
for&nbsp;future civilizations to understand our own—

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.160
just as stone tools have&nbsp;taught us about the Stone Age.&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.573
Losing these ubiquitous strands of polymers

00:03:25.573 --> 00:03:28.868
could mean losing a human era to antiquity.

00:03:28.868 --> 00:03:31.534
Archival voice: …are only two&nbsp;of the many plastics now in...

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:34.983
[TV static, followed by click]

00:03:35.078 --> 00:03:39.615
[Music]

